From 6a975008d9c071d342d6998b5c61b2e58b8f7695 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 19:58:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] Lip-sync character scenes to the narration
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The character engine and the lip-sync engine were disjoint branches of
the job runner: one generated a talking character who never spoke, the
other synced footage the pipeline never made. This joins them.

animateSceneKeyframes now optionally cuts each shot to its exact scene
length and syncs it to that scene's slice of the narration. One speaker
per shot — which is the only arrangement the identity lock can hold
anyway, since sceneKeyframePrompt anchors a single face and a second
face in frame drifts. That constraint is also what makes the whole
approach work without multi-face lip sync: because every scene is
generated from its own keyframe, there is never more than one mouth to
sync.

Scene starts are accumulated from scene durations rather than read off
cue indices. The storyboard-resume path rebuilds ScriptScene with
synthetic cue numbers, and slicing on those would hand shots the wrong
seconds of audio.

The sync runs inside the existing SCENE_CONCURRENCY window, so wall
clock grows by about one sync per scene divided by three rather than
one per scene.

Synced shots are flagged in the sceneMap. That flag is load-bearing:
without it the compositor seeks into, loops, swaps or splits the
footage, none of which raises an error and all of which desync the
mouth.

Failure is soft here and nowhere else in this path: the shot already
exists and has already been paid for, so a failed sync ships the
unsynced shot, unflagged, instead of failing the job.

lipSyncClip is a thin wrapper, not a provider adapter — the scene
generators cannot import the job runner without a cycle. Lip sync stays
pinned to Replicate; giving it the full VideoGenProviderDef treatment
is its own job, and starting it here would leave two half-migrations.

Gated by the existing lipSync kill switch, shared with the
upload-driven engine because both spend on the same model.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
